    {
      "name": "formats-yaml/alias-expansion/nested-anchor-fan-out-exceeds-expansion-limit",
      "spec": "docs/02-document-model.md#241-bounding-alias-expansion",
      "operation": "parse",
      "purpose": "error-case",
      "input": {
        "format": "yaml",
        "declared_max_alias_expansion": 50,
        "text": "a: &a leaf\nb: &b {p: *a, q: *a, r: *a, s: *a}\nc: &c {p: *b, q: *b, r: *b, s: *b}\nd: &d {p: *c, q: *c, r: *c, s: *c}\ne: &e {p: *d, q: *d, r: *d, s: *d}\ntop: *e\n"
      },
      "expect": {
        "ok": false,
        "diagnostics": [
          {
            "path": "$",
            "code": "document.limit.alias-expansion"
          }
        ]
      },
      "comment": "Five anchored definitions, each referring to the previous one four times -- the amplifying shape D-18 exists to stop, kept deliberately small. Expansion factors under \u00a72.4.1's definition, working outward: E(a)=1/1=1.00, E(b)=5/5=1.00, E(c)=21/5=4.20, E(d)=85/5=17.00, E(e)=341/5=68.20. E(e) exceeds the declared maximum of 50 (which is also the reference default), so the input MUST be rejected with document.limit.alias-expansion. Note what is NOT the reason for rejection: 341 materialized value slots is nowhere near any node cap, and the reference accepts this input today -- that acceptance, at a ~68x amplification an attacker can replay indefinitely, is exactly the defect omnist-spec#75 reports. Per D-19 the check is computed from the anchor/alias graph before expanding, so a conformant reader refuses this without materializing the 341 slots."
    },
